11.7.2 An overview of the system with 2 separate cabins moving independently inside the shaft is given below:
a) The lift group must be planned as a multiple car system.  The cars must operate independently and use the same shaft. The lift cars are arranged one above the other and run independently;
b) Both cars use the same guide rails.  The cars can move towards each other and are also able to move away from each other;
c) Each of the 2 lifts has its own drive system and counterweight.  Each counterweight runs on its own guide rails. Both lifts have their own safety equipment where required. Both cars use the same landing doors;
d) The suspension of the cars is dependent on the lift speed and travel height.  It can be 1 : 1 or 2 : 1;
e) Measures for rope compensation can be designed with compensation chains or belts to reach the required traction.  For speeds above 2.5 m/s tensioned compensation rope systems should be used;
f) The call assignments will be undertaken by destination control system.  The destination call will be set onto an input station (terminal) in the lobby in front of the lifts or in its proximity.  Therefore, pushbuttons for call assignment in the car are not required for passenger use.  Hidden COP is provided for fireman or special operation. The cars can serve floors with adequate distance directly above each other;
g) The minimum required safety distance depends on contract speed of the lifts.  An independent working safety controller guarantees the prevention of a collision in any operating condition;
h) Depending on the type of suspension and shaft dimensions, the drives are distributed in one, two or three machine room levels; and j) This system is designed for safety integrity level (SIL) 3 as defined in PESSRAL.
NOTE – Programmable electronic systems in safety related applications for lifts (PESSRAL) is included in the accepted standard [D-5A(21)].

