3.2 Based on source of generation, solid waste can be broadly categorized as follows:
a) Domestic waste — It is generated in residential units, commercial and educational entities; and consists of food wastes, paper, cardboard, plastics, textiles, leather, yard wastes, wood, glass, metals, ashes, special wastes (for example, bulky items, consumer electronics, white goods, batteries, oil and tyres), and household hazardous wastes including domestic sanitary, expired/discarded household usage chemicals, paints, automobiles batteries, household cleaners, swimming pool chemicals, and domestic e-waste;
b) Industrial waste — It is generated in manufacturing units, fabrication plants, construction sites, power and chemical plants; and consists of packaging, hazardous wastes, ashes, special wastes, wood, steel, concrete, dirt, etc;
c) Institutional and commercial waste — It is generated in schools, hospitals, prisons, government centers, stores, hotels, restaurants, markets, office buildings, etc; and consists of domestic wastes, paper, cardboard, plastics, wood, glass, metals, special wastes and hazardous wastes; and NATIONAL BUILDING CONSTRUCTION STANDARDS 2026
d) Horticulture waste — It is generated in landscaping, parks, orchards, dairies, and consists of street sweepings; landscape and tree trimmings; etc.

