3.1.2.1 An equipment room is an environmentally controlled centralized space for telecom equipment that usually
house equipment of higher complexity than telecommunication rooms and also usually houses a main or intermediate cross-connect.  Any or all of the functions of a telecommunications room may be provided in an equipment room.  ERs differ from TRs in the way, that, ERs are generally considered to serve a building, campus, tenant, or SP, whereas TRs serve a floor area of a building.  In some cases, an ER may also contain the EF or it may serve as TR.  ERs may be connected to backbone pathways that run both within and between buildings.
Although an ER usually serves an entire building, many building designs may use more than one ER in order to provide separate facilities for different types of equipment and services or redundant facilities and disaster recovery strategies.
The initial assessment for design and specifications for an ER shall be based on detailed information about the site, including:
a) User requirements;
b) Telecom pathway locations;
c) Service providers’ requirements;
d) Environment/facility conditions; and e) Building requirements.
