22.3 Requirements for Life Cycle Assessment
In the context of building with timber, LCA may be required for the following purposes:
a) Decision-Making Support — Providing building owners and stakeholders with data on the environmental and sustainability aspects of timber, aiding informed decision-making;
b) Design Evaluation — Offering insights into material selection and environmental performance across different design possibilities;
c) Product Comparison — Demonstrating the environmental advantages of timber in comparison to alternative building materials;
d) Environmental Benefit Evaluation — Helping assess the ecological benefits of innovative timber products or policies; and e) Performance Benchmarking — Comparing the environmental performance of timber buildings against established benchmarks and sustainability goals.

9.2.2.7 Design assumptions
The following assumptions shall be made in design of confined masonry wall panels subjected to axial and/or flexural loads:
a) masonry behaves like a homogeneous material;
b) strain distribution across the cross-section of the wall varies linearly;
c) tensile stresses are resisted only by reinforcing steel (masonry and concrete tensile resistance is ignored);
d) bond is perfect between steel reinforcement and adjacent concrete;
e) maximum compressive strains in masonry (εm) and concrete (εc) shall be taken equal to 0.004 0 and 0.003 0, respectively; and cm  NATIONAL BUILDING CONSTRUCTION STANDARDS 2026
the stress-strain curve of masonry is linear, unless a more accurate relation is determined by experimental study of masonry prisms.

