In this fourth revision of this Section, Subsections 5A and 5B have been merged into a single Section 5, ‘Structural concrete’, and the following additional changes are made:
a) Special concretes, namely self-compacting concrete, high-performance concrete, and steel fibre reinforced concrete, which were earlier covered in 16A, 16B and Annex A of the erstwhile NBC, are now included in Annex B, Annex C, and Annex D respectively;
b) Informative Annex (Annex A) on updated provisions regarding materials for making concrete is introduced;
c) Criteria for structural safety of tall concrete buildings is introduced in Annex E. This provides prescriptive requirements for design of reinforced concrete tall buildings. The following salient aspects, which are based on the prescriptive approach, are addressed in the Annex E:
1) Structural systems admissible for tall building in different seismic zones;
2) General requirements including: (i) height limitations of different structural systems, (ii) elevation and plan aspect ratios, (iii) lateral drift, (iv) storey stiffness and strength, (v) modes of vibration, (vi) floor systems, (vii) materials, and (viii) progressive collapse mechanism;
3) Wind and seismic effects: (i) load combinations, and (ii) acceptable serviceability criteria for lateral accelerations;
4) Methods of structural analysis to be adopted, and section properties (in cracked and uncracked states) of reinforced concrete member to be considered in analysis;
5) Structural design aspects for various applicable structural systems;
6) Issues to be considered in design of foundations; and 7) Systems needed for structural health monitoring. The upcoming fifth revision of IS 456 introduces a holistic design philosophy that emphasizes that concrete structures shall not only be strong but also safe, durable, serviceable under expected loads, and resilient in the face of extreme and accidental events.

