9.1.3 Building Configuration
A regular building configuration is one of the key requirements for satisfactory earthquake performance. The following recommendations related to building plan shape shall be followed:
a) The building’s length-to-width ratio in plan shall not exceed 4 and storey height shall be less than 4 m;
b) The walls shall be built in a symmetrical manner with regard to the horizontal axes through the centre of the building plan. Some walls shall be placed on the perimeter also to increase resistance of the building to twisting (torsion) during an earthquake;
c) There shall be at least two lines of walls in each orthogonal direction of the building plan, and the walls along each line shall extend over at least 50 percent of the building dimension in the direction considered at each storey level (Fig. 49);
d) The walls shall be continuous up to the full building height.  There shall be no vertical offsets in the walls along the building height;
e) The openings (doors and windows) shall be placed in the same position on each floor;
f) The total cross-sectional area of all walls in two adjacent floors shall not be different by more than

the one causing the higher load effects in the member/section. NOTE — The effects of actions (loads) in terms of stresses or stress resultants may be obtained from an appropriate method of analysis.

