6.3.4.1 Moment resisting frames
Rigid beam-column connections shall be formed using Type A, Type B, or Type C systems, depending on the required ductility level and building importance. All systems shall include structural topping for diaphragm action in accordance with 6.3.6:
a) Type-A frames — Type A frames shall consist of precast beam units between columns/column corbels, where columns can be cast-in-place or precast (see Fig. 2). These shall be achieved by adopting either one or a combination of the following arrangements:
1) System A1 — This system consists of single or multi-storey columns fixed at the base, supporting simply supported beams on corbels or directly above the columns. The slab units shall be simply supported between the ledges or above the beams;
2) System A2 — This system consists of single storey columns fixed at the base with provision of seating notch or temporary supports or permanent corbels for beams. Depending on design requirements, beams and slabs shall be designed as simply supported for temporary loading and made continuous for permanent loading;
3) System A3 — This system consists of single or multi-storey columns fixed at the base and staggered joints between the columns at other grid levels with corbels to support the beams. Staggering of columns shall be arranged such that only 50 percent of all columns are spliced at any given storey level. Depending on design requirements, beams and slabs shall be designed as simply supported for temporary loading and made continuous for permanent loading; and 4) System A4 — This system consists of 3 or 4 storeys columns fixed at the base with corbels to support the beams. Depending on design requirements, beams and slabs shall be designed as simply supported for temporary loading and made continuous for permanent loading. PART C  STRUCTURAL DESIGN — SECTION 7 PREFABRICATED CONCRETE CONSTRUCTION FIG.

11.5.2 Requirements
When a plastic method of analysis is used, all of the following conditions shall be satisfied, unless adequate ductility of the structure and plastic rotation capacity of its members and connections are established for the design loading conditions by other means of evaluation:
a) The yield stress of the grade of the steel used shall not exceed 450 MPa;
b) The stress-strain characteristics of the steel shall not be significantly different from those obtained for steels complying with the accepted standard [C-6A(6)] or equivalent and shall be such as to ensure complete plastic moment redistribution. The stress-strain diagram shall have a plateau at the yield stress, extending for at least six times the yield strain. The ratio of the tensile strength to the yield stress specified for the grade of the steel shall not be less than 1.2. The elongation on a gauge length complying with the accepted standard [C-6A(6)] shall not be less than 15 percent; and the steel shall exhibit strain-hardening capability. Steels conforming to the accepted standard [C-6A(6)] shall be deemed to satisfy the above requirements;
c) The members used shall be hot-rolled or fabricated using hot-rolled plates and sections;
d) The cross section of members not containing plastic hinges should be at least that of compact section (see 10.8.2), unless the members meet the strength requirements from elastic analysis;
e) Where plastic hinges occur in a member, the proportions of its cross section should not exceed the limiting values for plastic section given in 10.8.2;
f) The cross section should be symmetrical about its axis perpendicular to the axis of the plastic hinge rotation; and g) The members shall not be subject to impact loading, requiring fracture assessment or fluctuating loading, requiring a fatigue assessment [see 19].

