23.2 Principles of Sustainable Steel Construction
a) The structural steel design should provide a structure capable of fulfilling its intended function while sustaining the specified loads for its intended life with due regard to sustainability, robustness, economy, health and safety. Any expected change in the intended function during its life time should also be considered;
b) In environmentally sustainable construction all stake holders should consider the broader and holistic implications of the decision, which include the whole life cycle sustainability, impacts on other parts of the building and the project, externalities, maintenance, societal cost and consequences, without impinging upon safety and efficiency of the structure;
c) All parties in the supply chain for structural steelwork should take practical steps to reduce the greenhouse gas emissions of the processes over which they have control, thus contributing to sustainable construction.
Further, construction operations should avoid/minimize polluting the environment with dust, wastewater, chemicals and leaching debris;
d) Steelmaking should make full use of available secondary material (scrap) globally;
e) Structural steelwork should be designed, detailed, and constructed to minimize material use and wastage of material and facilitate its recovery for reuse at its end-of-life, as a part of the circular economy, to the extent that this is reasonably practicable; and f) The design should consider practical aspects of construction to avoid repair and rework.

