23.4.2 Minimizing Material Consumption through Design
Minimizing material consumption through design may be achieved by:
a) Avoiding over-specification of design loads;
b) Optimizing spans and grids: and adapting the building layout to take full advantage of the structural steelwork’s attributes;
c) Minimizing complex load paths and transfers in order to maximize direct load paths to ground;
d) Ensuring that structural deflection and vibration criteria, coordination zones and member depths are appropriate and allow for efficient structural solutions that avoid excessive material use;
e) Aiming to design for utilizations as close to 1.0 as practically possible, with due regard to permanent and temporary conditions;
f) Specifying higher strength steel grades in members or part of members not governed by serviceability criteria;
g) Considering key load paths, anticipated connection types and accurate loading criteria, in order to facilitate lean design principles;
h) Avoiding the over rationalization of steel member sizes to ensure efficient use of material, with due consideration of minimum economic quantities for procurement;
j) Reviewing section sizes and minimizing weight, whilst complying with safe methods of erection and fire protection requirements; and k) Semi-continuous design, which assumes that the joints transfer moments from the beam to the column and allow rotation between the connected members as the load is resisted.
NOTE — Historically, steelwork design and fabrication has mainly been driven by cost however, sustainability and particularly the climate emergency, are likely to change this with more clients willing or mandated to pay more for sustainable and low carbon buildings.
Reducing the carbon impact of steel structures through lean and less conservative design is technically achievable but can require more design effort and more complex fabrication.
