5.4.2.3 Discomfort glare
Glazing products with light transmission lower than 50 percent can ameliorate discomfort glare.  These products decrease the sky luminance components but permanently reduce the admission of daylight.  Alternatively, shading devices, internal or external, movable or fixed, may be used.
Other methods of reducing the problems of glare should be considered, including, a) installation of windows in more than one wall to raise the general background illumination and, in so doing, to reduce the contrast between a window and its surrounding surfaces;
b) use of light coloured matt finishes for the window frames and the surrounding surfaces;
c) splayed reveals, to assist in reducing the contrast between the window and its surroundings;
d) use of slender glazing bars and transoms of high reflectance; and e) lowering window sills to allow increased illumination to enter, which increases the adaptation level and reduces the likelihood of discomfort glare.

