7.6.1 Design Philosophy
The following factors shall be considered in designing for stability of a precast structure:
a) The overall structural system shall be checked for global and local stability under all applicable loading conditions including temporary and final stages;
b) Precast structural systems shall be designed to resist wind and all applicable lateral loads;
c) Cross walls or sway frames shall be so arranged to provide lateral stability;
d) When lateral loads are low, stability shall be verified using a notional horizontal force equal to 1.5 percent of total vertical load, as shown in Fig. 12;
e) Lateral stability shall be maintained during all construction and erection stages. Temporary bracing, shoring, or props shall be detailed and installed to match the staged loading conditions. A temporary NATIONAL BUILDING CONSTRUCTION STANDARDS 2026 All dimensions in millimetres.
FIG. 14 MOMENT AND SHEAR DISTRIBUTION WIDTHS FOR HCS
works scheme including propping layout, sequencing and dismantling plan shall be submitted and approved before execution;
f) Inverted T and L-beams shall be analysed for asymmetric loading during both construction and service stages, using all applicable load combinations;
g) The strength of grout shall be at least 50 percent of the design strength or as per design requirement before application of any other loads (temporary or permanent) on the element. Stress and cracking shall be evaluated and verified before proceeding;
h) Long-span and high-rise precast structures shall include engineered bracing and verified stability measures during all construction and service stages; and j) Temporary anchors shall be designed for proper alignment and safety of the elements. Appropriate washers shall be used.

One way of preventing progressive collapse of external wall panels is to design the combined precast wall panels of entire width as a beam of depth equal to the floor height for the impact loading of upper floors and provide reinforcement accordingly at the top and bottom of all precast wall panels at each floor. To make reinforcement continuous across joints, top and bottom bars projected into joints between wall panels shall be joined with lapping bars;
e) It is necessary to ensure that any local damage to a structure does not spread to other parts of the structure remote from the point of mishap and that the overall stability is not impaired, but it may not be necessary to stiffen all parts of the structure against local damage or collapse in the immediate vicinity of a mishap, unless the design briefs specially require this to be done. The requirements as specified at 8.5 shall be followed to prevent progressive collapse of the structure;
f) Additional protection may be required in respect of damage from vehicles. Further, it is necessary to consider the effect of damage to or displacement of a load-bearing member by an uncontrolled vehicle. It is strongly recommended that important structural members are adequately protected by concrete kerbs or similar method. In areas with possibility of passenger vehicular impact, the precast components shall be designed for an un-factored load of 27 kN, in any direction, acting at heights between 450 mm and
700 mm on an area not larger than 300 mm × 300 mm to produce maximum load effect. This load is not
required to act concurrently with any handrail or guard rail system loads; and g) In all aspects of erection that affect structural design, it is essential that the designer shall maintain a close liaison with the builder/contractor regarding the erection procedures to be followed.

