(Foreword, Clauses 6.5.1 and G-6.2.2) GUIDELINE FOR 3D PRINTED CONCRETE STRUCTURES B-1 GENERAL This annex provides an overview of the materials, methods and tests associated with concrete construction using 3D printing for various applications. The provisions contained herein are based on the current state of knowledge and practice in 3D printed concrete construction. In addition to these guidelines, reference shall be made to the applicable existing Indian standards and relevant research publications. Ongoing research at the national and international levels is expected to generate design-related data, which may subsequently be utilized for the purpose of standardization. B-2 METHODOLOGY AND SCOPE The following points are considered while defining the application and performance guidelines:
a) Printing technique — This document considers only extrusion-based 3D printing technology, where layers are deposited one over the other to construct a structure;
b) Design freedom — 3D printing allows for flexibility in the geometry and form. The shape, size and topology of any 3D printed concrete element for any application shall not be restricted. However, the performance requirements for 3D printing need to be satisfied;
c) Material — The material shall be printable and buildable. As per the current state of knowledge of concrete for 3D printing, the limits on the usage of cementitious materials as given in good practice [C-7(6)] and Part C ‘Structural Design, Section 5 Structural Concrete’ of this NBCS would not be applicable for 3D printed concrete. The assessment of 3D printability of any concrete mixture needs to be carried out as per the available specialist literature on the subject. The mechanical, durability and long-term properties of the printable material shall be determined, as applicable. In the absence of a well-established procedure for preparation of test specimens for such evaluations, reference shall be made to recognized specialized literature.

