10.3.8 Other Methods of Accelerated Hardening
Accelerated hardening of concrete may also be achieved by one or more of the following techniques:
a) Use of Construction Chemicals — Suitable construction chemicals may be used for promoting early hardening;
b) Consolidation by Spinning — This method shall generally be employed in centrifugal moulding of pipes and similar units. The spinning action removes excess water, effects consolidation, and permits earlier demoulding;
c) Pressed Concrete — This method shall be suitable for the fabrication of small or large products at high rates of production. Presses in the range of 100 t to 200 t capacity shall be used to compress the wet concrete in rigid moulds, thereby expelling excess water. This results in early handling and produces dense, wear-resistant concrete;
d) Vacuum Treatment — In this method, surplus air and water shall be removed from the freshly placed concrete (for example, slabs and similar elements). A suction of up to 70 percent of an atmosphere shall be applied for a period of 20 min to 30 min per cm thickness of the units; and e) Consolidation by Shock — This method shall be suitable for small concrete units placed in strong moulds and subjected to repeated dropping from a height. The number of shocks required to expel excess water and air may vary from 6 to 20 and the height of drop may be up to half the depth of the mould.

